## VERDICT: **FAIL**

### FOUND — two P1 regressions

**D139 (P1, NEW).** R9b's third disjunct `!NEGATION_AUX.test(c.slice(0, n))` treats a negator preceded by any finite auxiliary as out-of-scope. English disagrees: *"There is no record ACME was archived."* is the ordinary zero-relativizer complement and its negator scopes over everything. The belt fires on the **true answer**, and `legacyProseFallback` (index.ts:5739) replaces it with *"I can't actually do that from chat — nothing was changed"* — a mutation refusal answering a read-only question. **18/75** on my corpus; **24/24** canonical completion-word company names. Survives at d34af15, fbafded, a559f8f, 9535f0b, 52e830f — destroyed only here and at pre-negation d724d8c. Isolated by two controls (relativizer variant and non-aux lead-in both survive) and by reverting that one term.

**D142 (P1, NEW — not in the prior checkpoint).** D138's `commandForContradiction` strips the matched label **unconditionally**. When the label *is* an opposite-family verb (a company named "Restore"/"ReStore"/"Archive"/"Delete"), the strip removes the founder's own verb → reply `restore` against a pending **archive** arms **`archiveCompanyIds`**: a destructive bind on the opposite intent, no LLM in the loop. Prior SHAs return `null`. **The same commit already implements the correct rule** — D136's ambiguity dead-end — on the ordinal path only.

### ROOT CAUSE / SYSTEMIC IMPACT
Both are the same class: *a fix that widens a rule without an adversarial corpus for the specific term it adds.* R9b was adopted because #19 measured it — correctly — but what was measured was its score on one 61-case corpus, never that one disjunct against the shape it breaks. Same-class search found **D141** (P2, **pre-existing at all 7 revisions** — `NEGATED_CLAUSE` has no `nobody`/`neither`/`nowhere`/`nor`/`few`/`hardly`; *"not archived, nor was it deleted"* is destroyed), **D144** (P3, new — one fabrication d34af15 caught is now missed), and `safeOptionLabel` returning `null` for real names starting with a completion word (pre-existing; D138 closed only the matcher half of that class).

